Description: hw/misc: Add the Chumby Classic's board lines
 The stock kernel's sense1 driver handles the Chumby's board-level lines:
 the LCD backlight through the PWM's duty cycle (/proc/sys/sense1/
 brightness), the LCD's power on keypad column 2, the speaker
 amplifier's mute on column 4 (spkmute, high to mute), the headphone
 jack's switch on row 3 (hpin, low while plugged in) and the top button
 on row 0.
 .
 Add a chumby-io device that models these lines, for the board to wire
 up:
 .
 - the backlight level and the LCD's power make the LCD controller's
   brightness, so the screen dims with the backlight and goes black
   when either is off. Until the guest first programs the PWM the
   backlight is full, so kernels without a backlight driver show their
   screen.
 - headphones are plugged in and out with the "headphones" property, and
   the top button pressed with "button".
 - a key, F12 unless "button-key" names another QKeyCode or none, holds
   the button while it is down, from any display's keyboard or QMP's
   send-key and input-send-event. F12 has no use in the stock firmware,
   which has no keyboard, nor in QEMU's own displays, so the button
   works in a window out of the box. Other keys pass on to the guest's
   keyboard. A keyboard, plugged in or given with -device, puts its
   handler first, so the button's is put back in front.
 - when a key is pressed that no keyboard takes, a usb-kbd is plugged
   into the root hub, as a user would plug one in to type
   ("usb-keyboard", on by default). The port's reset empties the
   keyboard's queue, so the keys are held until the guest has configured
   the keyboard and its driver has had half a second to open it, then
   typed one every 20 ms: the keyboard queues only 16 key changes. A
   subsection, sent while that keyboard is plugged in, saves the replay
   of held keys and its timer and plugs the keyboard in on load, before
   the keyboard's own state, which comes later in the stream.
 - changes are reported with the CHUMBY_* QMP events, and
   query-chumby-state returns the lot, with the accelerometer's input
   (read from the accelerometer, so a value set on it directly shows)
   and the machine's identity.
 .
 Speaker mute and headphones are also GPIO outputs, for an audio model
 to use.
